FINDINGS:
1. [BLOCKER] src/bytecode/vm.rs:2630 — `run_catch` pushes a scope at 2617, drives the catch frame (which already truncates locals to `locals_base` in `unwind_frame` at 1105 / `finish_top_frame` at 2053), then unconditionally `pop()`s again — every `catch` removes two scopes instead of one, corrupting the enclosing function locals while top-level programs mask it because their names live in globals — remove the explicit `pop()` and rely on the truncate, matching tree-walker `run_catch_body` at src/vm.rs:1232 which truncates once with no extra pop.
2. [MAJOR] src/vm.rs:1128 — `self.run_catch_body(catch_var.as_deref(), catch_body)?;` early-returns on a failing catch so `run_finally_body` at 1131 never runs; bytecode mirrors it at src/bytecode/vm.rs:2522 with `run_catch(...).and_then(|_| run_finally(...))` — a `try/catch/finally` whose catch fails skips the same `try`'s `finally`, contradicting the phase's own "finally is still owed" rule (SPEC.md:464) — capture the catch error, run the finally, then propagate.
3. [MAJOR] tests/test_loop_control.rb:508 — `a break in an object body leaves the loop` exists but no `skip` in an object body (nor in a `test` body) exists on either VM, and tests/bytecode_vm_test.rs:829 carries only the break shape — the `Skip` path through `LoopOwner`/object-body frames is a new branch with no test on either side — add `loop control: a skip in an object body advances the loop` plus a `flow/a-skip-in-an-object-body` corpus program compared on both VMs.
